  "title": "CAR-T cell therapy for canines with B-cell lymphoma | College of Veterinary Medicine",
  "text": "CAR-T cell therapy for canines with B-cell lymphoma | College of Veterinary Medicine\nSkip to main content\nGo to the University of Minnesota Twin Cities home page\nOne Stop\nMyU\nSearch\nCollege of Veterinary Medicine\nHospitals & care\nCurrent students\nMenu\nAbout us\nAbout\nInclusive Excellence\nInclusive excellence overview\nCommitment to inclusive excellence\nDiversity, Equity and Inclusion committee\nCommunity\nResources\nReporting bias or sexual misconduct\nInclusive Excellence Symposium\nEmployment\nOur faculty & staff\nStrategic plan 2023-2026\nImpact report\nEducation\nWhat we offer\nDoctor of Veterinary Medicine\nGraduate Programs\nDual degree programs\nDVM/MPH\nInternship & Residency Programs\nAccreditation\nDepartments\nDepartments overview\nDepartment of Veterinary and Biomedical Sciences\nVeterinary Clinical Sciences\nVeterinary Population Medicine\nDepartment of Veterinary Population Medicine\nOur faculty & staff\nResearch\nContact us\nTeaching\nSTEMMA\nCenters and programs\nCenters & programs overview\nCenter for Animal Health and Food Safety\nCanine Brain Tumor Program\nClinical Investigation Center\nCommunity medicine initiative\nJohn Fetrow Dairy Education center\nLeatherdale Equine Center\nMinnesota Urolith Center\nSecure Food Systems\nSecure Poultry Supply\nSwine program\nThe Raptor Center\nVeterinary Diagnostic Laboratory\nResearch\nResearch overview\nResearch news\nResearch office\nRIDE Summit\nRIDE Seminar Series\nAnimal Cancer Care & Research\nResearch labs\nResearch laboratories home\nBlood and Laboratory Support Services\nCanine genetics lab\nClinical Pathology Lab\nEquine Genetics and Genomics\nMinnesota Center for Prion Research and Outreach (MNPRO)\nMinnesota Urolith Center\nModiano Lab\nMycoplasma Research Laboratory\nResearch training programs\nNews & Events\nMedia Room\nProfiles\nEvents\nAlumni & Giving\nGive\nAlumni\nContact us\nBreadcrumb\nHome\nVeterinary Clinical Investigation Center\nCurrent clinical trials\nCAR-T cell therapy for canines with B-cell lymphoma\nPrincipal Investigator\nMike Henson, DVM, PhD, Diplomate ACVIM\nProfessor, Oncology, Veterinary Clinical Sciences\n[email protected]\nCo-investigator\nCaitlin Feiock, DVM\nAssistant Professor, Veterinary Clinical Sciences\nResearch Manager, Clinical Investigation Center\n[email protected]\n612-624-2485\nContact\nKelly Reid, LVT\nResearch Professional 2\n[email protected]\n612-625-7645\nI'm interested\nCategory:\nCancer/Oncology\nSpecies:\nDog\nCurrent status:\nOpen and enrolling\nStudy title\nEvaluation of xenogeneic CAR-T cell therapy for canine CD20+ B cell lymphoma\nPurpose of study\nThe current standard of care for canine B-cell lymphoma is a multi-agent (CHOP) chemotherapy protocol, which require weekly trips to the vet over approximately 5 months, costs $7k-12k, and puts 80-90% of dogs into remission for 9-12 months before relapse. Canine B-cell lymphoma resembles human non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma, a cancer now successfully treated with Chimeric Antigen Receptor T cells (CAR-T cells). In this study, LEAH Labs is sponsoring an pilot trial to test the safety of a CAR-T cell therapy product in dogs with B-cell lymphoma; with the hopes to find a more cost-effective and efficacious therapy for dogs with this disease.\nEligibility criteria\nEligible dogs must match these criteria:\nbody weight of at least 10kgs (22lbs)\nany breed, except boxers\nClinical presentation:\nPeripheral multicentric lymphoma (minimum of 4 target peripheral lymph nodes evaluable)\nHave naïve or relapsed disease, substage A only\nfine needle aspirate (in-house or reference lab) consistent with lymphoblastic/high grade lymphoma\nperformance status 0-1\nConcurrent medications of note:\nImmunomodulatory drugs (Apoquel, mycophenolate, cyclosporine, azathioprine) are not allowed\nCytopoint\nis allowable and can be maintained for the study period\nPrednisone use:\nnot allowed if it's used for substage B disease\npatient must be able to taper off 72 hours prior to CAR-T administration and remain off for duration of study\nProcedures\nScreening visit(s):\nPhysical exam (PE), blood and urine tests, fine needle aspirates (FNA), lymph node measurements, chest x-rays, abdominal ultrasound\nPre-treatment visits (Days -5 to -2):\nPE, blood for CBC, fluids (before and after treatment), lymph node measurements, support medication (in hospital and sent home with), Pentostatin bolus\nDay -3:\nalong with above, IV catheter placed, Cyclophosphamide and Furosemide infusion; overnight hospital stay for observation\nCAR-T administration (Day 0):\nPE, blood tests, lymph node measurements, IV catheter place, CAR-T infusion and injection in lymph nodes\nFollow-up visits (Day +7, Day +21):\nPE, blood tests, lymph node measurements; UA, chest x-rays and abdominal ultrasound on day +21\nPost CAR-T visits (2 and 3 months):\nPE, blood tests, chest x-rays, abdominal ultrasound\nUnscheduled visits:\nif your dog needs to be seen between the study visits above, they may consist of a PE, blood and urine tests, lymph node measurements, FNA\nAt any time the dog can be hospitalized in ICU for supportive fluids or other procedures as needed\nNote, urinalysis may be added to recheck visits if clinical signs indicate it is needed\nCosts\nThis study is fully funded and will cover the costs of visits as outlined above, including the screening visit. A $2,000 stipend upon completion of the study or, If at any time, animal welfare in relation to disease or tumor progression necessitates early study departure. This stipend can only be used at the University of Minnesota Veterinary Medical Center for future cancer care and monitoring.\nAppropriate medications will be provided for your dog up to $4,000 if he/she develops any severe side effects due to the CAR-T treatment that require medical management, when care is provided at the University of Minnesota's Veterinary Medical Center.\nGlobal footer\nCollege of Veterinary Medicine\n612-624-6244\nFax: 612-624-8753\n1365 Gortner Avenue\nSt. Paul, MN, 55108\nResources\nAlumni\nCampus maps\nContact us\nCurrent students\nEmployment\nIntranets\nCollege of Veterinary Medicine\nVeterinary Medical Center\nFeedback\nWebsite feedback\nGive\nFollow us\nResearch ethics at the University of Minnesota\n. We are committed to protecting research participants, upholding ethical standards, and improving our practice at every step of our work.\nFor Students, Faculty, and Staff\nOne Stop\nMyU\n©\n2026\nRegents of the\nUniversity of Minnesota\n. All rights reserved. The University of Minnesota is an equal opportunity educator and employer.\nPrivacy Statement\nReport Web Accessibility Issues\nAbout us\nAbout\nInclusive Excellence\nInclusive excellence overview\nCommitment to inclusive excellence\nDiversity, Equity and Inclusion committee\nCommunity\nResources\nReporting bias or sexual misconduct\nInclusive Excellence Symposium\nEmployment\nOur faculty & staff\nStrategic plan 2023-2026\nImpact report\nEducation\nWhat we offer\nDoctor of Veterinary Medicine\nGraduate Programs\nDual degree programs\nDVM/MPH\nInternship & Residency Programs\nAccreditation\nDepartments\nDepartments overview\nDepartment of Veterinary and Biomedical Sciences\nVeterinary Clinical Sciences\nVeterinary Population Medicine\nDepartment of Veterinary Population Medicine\nOur faculty & staff\nResearch\nContact us\nTeaching\nSTEMMA\nCenters and programs\nCenters & programs overview\nCenter for Animal Health and Food Safety\nCanine Brain Tumor Program\nClinical Investigation Center\nCurrent clinical trials\nCanine Brain Tumor Program\nCompleted clinical studies\nFor investigators\nFor UMN & external collaborators\nFor CVM investigators\nAbout\nOur Staff\nContact us\nRegulatory information\nCommunity medicine initiative\nJohn Fetrow Dairy Education center\nJohn Fetrow Dairy Education Center overview\nDavis partner\nVeterinary student education\nFunding & support\nStaying at the center\nContact us\nOur faculty\nLeatherdale Equin